{"subjectId":"java","id":"linkedhashmap-treemap","title":"LinkedHashMap & TreeMap","group":"Collections Framework","order":45,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>LinkedHashMap</code> = <code>HashMap</code> + a doubly-linked list for predictable order (and a two-line LRU cache). <code>TreeMap</code> = a red-black tree for sorted keys and nearest-key queries.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>Map implementations compared</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>HashMap</code></th><th><code>LinkedHashMap</code></th><th><code>TreeMap</code></th><th><code>Hashtable</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Order</td><td>None</td><td>Insertion (or access)</td><td>Sorted by key</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td><code>get</code> / <code>put</code></td><td>O(1) avg</td><td>O(1) avg</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>O(1) avg</td></tr><tr><td><code>null</code> key</td><td>One</td><td>One</td><td>No (natural order)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td><code>null</code> values</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Thread-safe</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (coarse lock)</td></tr><tr><td>Key requirement</td><td><code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code></td><td><code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code></td><td><code>Comparable</code>/<code>Comparator</code></td><td><code>equals</code>/<code>hashCode</code></td></tr><tr><td>Use for</td><td>Default map</td><td>Stable order, LRU caches</td><td>Sorted output, ranges, floor/ceiling</td><td>Nothing new — legacy</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right\"></i><code>LinkedHashMap</code> orders</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; ins = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();\n        ins.put(\"banana\", 2);\n        ins.put(\"apple\", 1);\n        ins.put(\"cherry\", 3);\n        ins.put(\"banana\", 20);          // re-put does NOT move the key\n        System.out.println(ins);\n\n        // accessOrder = true: every get/put moves the entry to the end\n        Map&lt;String, Integer&gt; acc = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;(16, 0.75f, true);\n        acc.putAll(Map.of(\"a\", 1));\n        acc.put(\"b\", 2);\n        acc.put(\"c\", 3);\n        acc.get(\"a\");\n        System.out.println(acc);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{banana=20, apple=1, cherry=3}\n{b=2, c=3, a=1}</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>LRU cache in a few lines</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        LruCache&lt;Integer, String&gt; cache = new LruCache&lt;&gt;(2);\n        cache.put(1, \"one\");\n        cache.put(2, \"two\");\n        cache.get(1);                 // 1 is now most recently used\n        cache.put(3, \"three\");        // evicts 2, the least recently used\n        System.out.println(cache.keySet());\n    }\n}\n\nclass LruCache&lt;K, V&gt; extends LinkedHashMap&lt;K, V&gt; {\n    private final int capacity;\n\n    LruCache(int capacity) {\n        super(16, 0.75f, true);       // access order\n        this.capacity = capacity;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry&lt;K, V&gt; eldest) {\n        return size() &gt; capacity;     // called after every put\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[1, 3]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>This is the standard answer to “design an LRU cache” in Java. The from-scratch version (a <code>HashMap</code> of nodes + your own doubly-linked list) has the same O(1) <code>get</code>/<code>put</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary-tree\"></i><code>TreeMap</code> as a <code>NavigableMap</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeMap&lt;Integer, String&gt; grades = new TreeMap&lt;&gt;();\n        grades.put(90, \"A\");\n        grades.put(80, \"B\");\n        grades.put(70, \"C\");\n        grades.put(0, \"F\");\n\n        int score = 84;\n        System.out.println(score + \" -&gt; \" + grades.floorEntry(score).getValue());\n        System.out.println(grades.firstKey() + \" \" + grades.lastKey());\n        System.out.println(grades.floorKey(75) + \" \" + grades.ceilingKey(75));\n        System.out.println(grades.ceilingEntry(95));             // no key &gt;= 95\n        System.out.println(grades.headMap(80));                  // keys &lt; 80\n        System.out.println(grades.subMap(70, true, 90, false));  // [70, 90)\n        System.out.println(grades.descendingMap());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>84 -&gt; B\n0 90\n70 80\nnull\n{0=F, 70=C}\n{70=C, 80=B}\n{90=A, 80=B, 70=C, 0=F}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>floorKey(k)</code> / <code>floorEntry(k)</code></td><td>Greatest key <code>&lt;= k</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>ceilingKey(k)</code> / <code>ceilingEntry(k)</code></td><td>Least key <code>&gt;= k</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>lowerKey(k)</code> / <code>higherKey(k)</code></td><td>Strictly <code>&lt; k</code> / <code>&gt; k</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>firstEntry()</code> / <code>pollFirstEntry()</code></td><td>Smallest (and remove it)</td></tr><tr><td><code>headMap(k)</code> / <code>tailMap(k)</code> / <code>subMap(a, b)</code></td><td>Live range views</td></tr><tr><td><code>descendingMap()</code></td><td>Reverse-order view</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>All the <code>floor</code>/<code>ceiling</code> methods return <code>null</code> when no such key exists — unboxing that <code>null</code> into an <code>int</code> throws <code>NullPointerException</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you implement an LRU cache in Java?</summary><div><p>Extend <code>LinkedHashMap</code> with <code>accessOrder = true</code> and override <code>removeEldestEntry</code> to return <code>size() &gt; capacity</code>. Access order moves each touched entry to the tail; the eldest (head) is the least recently used and is evicted after a <code>put</code>. For thread safety wrap it or use a library such as Caffeine.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you choose TreeMap over HashMap?</summary><div><p>When you need keys in sorted order or queries like “nearest key below x” (<code>floorKey</code>), “all keys in a range” (<code>subMap</code>), min/max key — e.g. time-series lookups, interval scheduling, leaderboard ranges. The cost is O(log n) per operation instead of O(1).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What internal structure does TreeMap use?</summary><div><p>A red-black tree — a self-balancing binary search tree whose height stays ≤ 2·log₂(n+1), guaranteeing O(log n) <code>get</code>, <code>put</code>, <code>remove</code>. Ordering comes from <code>compareTo</code> or the supplied <code>Comparator</code>; <code>equals</code> is not used.</p></div></details></section>"}